"Change of Mailing Address | Central District of California | United States Bankruptcy Court (https://www.cacb.uscourts.gov/forms/change-mailing-address)\nciteturn1256view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1241view0\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 63\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: # cite1†United States Bankruptcy Court L3: \nL4: ## cite1†Central District of California L5: \nL6: # cite1†Hon. Julia W. Brand, Chief Judge • Kathleen J. Campbell, Clerk of Court L7: \nL8: ## Search form\nL9: \nL10: Search this site [Input: Enter search words]\nL11: \nL12: [Input]\nL13: \nL14: [Input] [Input]\nL15: \nL16: ## You are here\nL17: # Change of Mailing Address\nL18: \nL19: MANDATORY\nL20: \nL21: Form Type:\nL22: \nL23: Local Bankruptcy Rules Forms\nL24: \nL25: Form #:\nL26: \nL27: F 1002-1.3.CHANGE.ADDRESS\nL28: \nL29: Download Form (pdf version):\nL30: \nL31: cite2†Image: application/pdf cite3†F1002-1.3CHANGEADDRESSb.pdf L32: \nL33: Related Local Rule:\nL34: \nL35: cite4†Rule 1001-1 through 1073-1 L36: \nL37: Effective Date:\nL38: \nL39: 06/2015\nL40: \nL41: Search this site [Input: Search Forms]\nL42: \nL43: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL44: \nL45: [Input]\nL46: ## Forms Quicklinks\nL47: \nL48: Instruction Booklets\nL49: \nL50: cite5†Bankruptcy Forms for Individuals L51: cite6†Bankruptcy Forms for Non-Individuals L52: \nL53: * * *\nL54: \nL55: cite7†File a Claim L56: \nL57: * * *\nL58: \nL59: cite8†New and Revised L60: cite9†All Forms L61: \nL62: Your browser does not support Javascript. Your browser does not support Javascript.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSuperior Court of California County of Orange (https://www.occourts.org/system/files/statement_on_continuances.pdf)\nciteturn1256view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1239view3\",\"id\":316}); Total lines: 24\nL0@P0: \nL1@P0:  Superior Court of California\nL2@P0:  County of Orange\nL3@P0: Probate’s Statement on\nL4@P0: Continuances\nL5@P0: February 2021\nL6@P0: As most counsel are aware, the Court’s calendars have increased exponentially during\nL7@P0: the pandemic. Many of those calendars reflect multiple continuances for notes to be\nL8@P0: cleared. In an effort to reduce calendar size and clear the Court’s backlog, the following\nL9@P0: policy is now in effect:\nL10@P0: “If notes have not been cleared by the third continuance, and depending on the matter\nL11@P0: before the Court, the petition may be dismissed without prejudice and the party\nL12@P0: bringing the petition may be required to pay the filing fee to re-file or an appointed\nL13@P0: personal representative, conservator or guardian may be suspended and an OSC re\nL14@P0: removal set.”\nL15@P0: This action is not taken lightly. The number of continuances occurring due to failure to\nL16@P0: clear notes has begun to interfere with the court’s ability to process, schedule and\nL17@P0: approve the matters which otherwise could be more quickly set for hearing. Matters\nL18@P0: continued multiple times for failure to clear notes should not prevent the approval of\nL19@P0: petitions which have been properly submitted.\nL20@P0: We acknowledge that some delays are caused by the significant back log in approving\nL21@P0: orders, letters, etc. In those cases, we will try and continue the matter out far enough\nL22@P0: that those submitted documents can be reviewed and, hopefully, approved so that\nL23@P0: another appearance is not necessary.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSuperior Court of California County of Orange (https://www.occourts.org/system/files/trial-hearing-rules-probate.pdf)\nciteturn1256view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 10; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1239view3\",\"id\":317}); Total lines: 268\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: Revised: December 2021\nL2@P0: Superior Court of California\nL3@P0: County of Orange\nL4@P0: PROBATE DEPARTMENT\nL5@P0: HEARING AND TRIAL GUIDELINES\nL6@P0: Welcome to Probate. The Court recommends that all counsel and self-represented parties read and\nL7@P0: familiarize themselves with Orange County Local Court Rules, Division 6 (Probate Rules) and Titles 3 and\nL8@P0: 7 of the California Rules of Court (Civil and Probate Rules, respectively).\nL9@P0: To facilitate the progress of your case and assure its timely disposition, this Court has established\nL10@P0: the following guidelines:\nL11@P0: 1. Unless otherwise ordered by the Court, once contested, your matter will proceed through three\nL12@P0: court hearings;\nL13@P0: A. Trial Setting Conference (TSC);\nL14@P0: B. Mandatory Settlement Conference (MSC);\nL15@P0: C. Trial.\nL16@P0: 2. The Court expects counsel for all parties to maintain regular communication regarding hearing\nL17@P0: dates, progress of the case and settlement possibilities.\nL18@P0: 3. The Court believes in cooperative discovery and expects counsel to communicate in order to\nL19@P0: reduce the need for discovery motions. The prevailing party on discovery motions will generally\nL20@P0: be awarded costs and attorney fees.\nL21@P0: 4. The Court requires written notice of all hearing dates unless waived by all counsel or self\u0002represented parties.\nL22@P0: 5. The Court accepts the standards of liberal amendment of pleadings and expects counsel in most\nL23@P0: cases to stipulate to amend pleadings with a minimum response time of 10 days.\nL24@P0: 6. Counsel may not stipulate to a continuance of a TSC, MSC or Trial without Court approval. A\nL25@P0: stipulation must be submitted with a place for the Court’s signature. If all counsel/parties agree\nL26@P0: to the continuance, an ex parte application is not required. However, the stipulation should state\nL27@P0: the reason for the requested continuance.\nL28@P0: 7. The Court may provide tentative rulings on law and motion matters on the Court website. If the\nL29@P0: matter is resolved prior to the hearing date, please call the department where the matter is\nL30@P0-1: scheduled.2\nL31@P1: Revised: December 2021\nL32@P1: 8. In the event a matter settles before a court hearing, the parties must file a stipulation in writing\nL33@P1: signed by all the parties and counsel and in compliance with Code of Civil Procedure section 664.6\nL34@P1: which expressly sets forth the resolution of each pending petition; otherwise all parties and\nL35@P1: counsel must appear to have the matter placed on the record, or resolved by such other order as\nL36@P1: the Court may direct.\nL37@P1: TRIAL SETTING CONFERENCE\nL38@P1: 1. A Trial Setting Conference (TSC) may be set by the Court at the first hearing at which the\nL39@P1: matter is at issue.\nL40@P1: 2. All counsel and self-represented parties must complete a Joint Trial Setting Conference\nL41@P1: Statement (see optional local form L-1113) and file it at least five court days before the\nL42@P1: hearing.\nL43@P1: 3. At the Trial Setting Conference, the Court will review the case with counsel and self\u0002represented parties to determine whether a settlement conference should be scheduled,\nL44@P1: and when trial should be set.\nL45@P1: 4. The Trial Setting Conference may be continued only with the Court’s permission.\nL46@P1: MANDATORY SETTLEMENT CONFERENCE\nL47@P1: 1. A Mandatory Settlement Conference (MSC) will be required in all contested matters,\nL48@P1: unless excused by the court.\nL49@P1: 2. The court may order the matter to mediation pursuant to Breslin v Breslin (2021) 62\nL50@P1: Cal.App.5th 801. The parties should be prepared to discuss the possibility for settlement.\nL51@P1: 3. A settlement conference statement must be filed and served at least five court days before\nL52@P1: the MSC. Since MSC’s are conducted on Friday mornings, this means your statement must\nL53@P1: be filed no later than the previous Friday. Counsel must comply with California Rules of\nL54@P1: Court, Rules 3.1380 (b) and (c). Failure to comply with the court’s order will result in the\nL55@P1: setting of an Order to Show Cause Why Sanctions should not be ordered pursuant to CCP\nL56@P1: 177.5 and CCP 128.5\nL57@P1: 4. Trial counsel and all parties must personally appear at the MSC unless excused by the Court\nL58@P1-2: on a prior written request with notice to all other parties.3\nL59@P2: Revised: December 2021\nL60@P2: ISSUE CONFERENCE\nL61@P2: An Issue Conference, NOT INVOLVING THE COURT, will be required in all cases not less than 10\nL62@P2: days before trial, at which time counsel are to meet and confer and execute necessary documents\nL63@P2: as listed below. Counsel for the Petitioner must arrange the Issue Conference at a mutually\nL64@P2: agreeable time and location. This rule does not apply to cases involving establishment or\nL65@P2: termination of a Guardianship, but a trial brief is helpful to the Court in those cases, and all exhibits\nL66@P2: must be tagged per the procedure outlined herein. At the Issue Conference the parties must:\nL67@P2: 1. Exchange exhibits and inspect documents and diagrams. The parties must pre-mark\nL68@P2: exhibitsfor use during the trial per the attached instructions in the Trial Procedures\nL69@P2: section below. The parties must prepare a joint exhibit list. The parties are advised\nL70@P2: that the Digital Evidence Portal is now available and can be accessed on the court’s\nL71@P2: website. Ask the court clerk if the Digital Evidence Portal will be utilized in your\nL72@P2: case.\nL73@P2: 2. Stipulate to all facts amendable to stipulation. Prepare a joint list of stipulated\nL74@P2: facts.\nL75@P2: 3. Prepare a Joint Pretrial Statement of the Case, as set forth herein.\nL76@P2: 4. Prepare a Joint Witness List, excluding the names of impeachment witnesses, if\nL77@P2: any.\nL78@P2: 5. Exchange all motions in limine, if any.\nL79@P2: 6. Exchange trial briefs, if any. Trial briefs should not exceed 20 pages.\nL80@P2: 7. Execute the Joint Statement of Compliance. See attached.\nL81@P2: 8. Prepare exhibit notebooks to be submitted at trial. Do not file them with the\nL82@P2: clerk.\nL83@P2: The above items, including opposition to motions in limine, must be filed at least five court\nL84@P2: days before trial.\nL85@P2: NOTE: Failure to conduct the Issue Conference as required may result in sanctions.\nL86@P2: PRETRIAL STATEMENT\nL87@P2: (Not applicable to cases involving establishment or termination of a Guardianship or Limited\nL88@P2: Conservatorship)\nL89@P2: At least five court days before trial, the parties must file a Joint Pretrial Statement of the Case, or\nL90@P2-3: if after a good faith attempt preparation of a joint statement is not feasible, separate Pretrial4\nL91@P3: Revised: December 2021\nL92@P3: Statements of the Case. The parties are reminded the Joint Pretrial Statement is a summary of the\nL93@P3: allegations and defenses in the petitions and objections in the matters set for trial. It is not a trial\nL94@P3: brief. The Pretrial Statement must follow the form and contain the captions and information as\nL95@P3: listed below:\nL96@P3: 1. Party\nL97@P3: The names of the parties on whose behalf the statement is filed and counsel.\nL98@P3: 2. Substance of the Action\nL99@P3: Identify the petitions at issue and the filing date or registry of action for each petition.\nL100@P3: Proved a brief summary of the substance of the allegations and defenses presented and\nL101@P3: of the issues to be decided.\nL102@P3: 3. Time Estimate\nL103@P3: Provide the court with time estimates for trial and the manner in which each party and\nL104@P3: attorney intends to appear (ie. remotely or in person). The time estimate should include\nL105@P3: anticipated time for direct and cross examination of witnesses.\nL106@P3: 4. Controverted Issues and Statements of Fact\nL107@P3: The parties are ordered to prepare a plain and concise List of Controverted Issues and\nL108@P3: Statements of Fact. Include a statement of the requested prayers for relief.. For each\nL109@P3: listed issue, unless addressed in separately-filed trial briefs, this section to include the\nL110@P3: facts and legal authority supporting each party’s position on that issue.\nL111@P3: 5. Undisputed Facts\nL112@P3: A concise statement of all material facts not reasonably disputed. Counsel to make a good\nL113@P3: faith effort to stipulate to facts not reasonably disputable for incorporation into the trial\nL114@P3: record without the necessity of supporting testimony or exhibits.\nL115@P3: 6. Amendments, Dismissals\nL116@P3: A statement of requested or proposed amendments to pleadings or dismissals of parties,\nL117@P3: claims or defenses.\nL118@P3: 7. Bifurcation, Separate Trial of Issues\nL119@P3: A statement whether bifurcation of specific issues is feasible and desired.\nL120@P3: 8. Witnesses to be Called\nL121@P3: A list of all witnesseslikely to be called at trial, except for impeachment. Provide a separate\nL122@P3: copy of the Witness List for the Court’s use at trial.\nL123@P3: 9. Previous Motions\nL124@P3-4: A list of all previous motions made in the action or proceeding and their disposition.5\nL125@P4: Revised: December 2021\nL126@P4: SUPERIOR COURT OF CALIFORNIA\nL127@P4: COUNTY OF ORANGE\nL128@P4: CASE NAME: _________________________ CASE NO. ______________________________\nL129@P4: STATEMENT OF COMPLIANCE\nL130@P4: This Statement of Compliance must be executed and filed by all counsel at the Pretrial Conference.\nL131@P4: 1. Counsel have inspected all exhibits and diagrams, the exhibits have been tagged, marked, and are\nL132@P4: “bates labeling” numbered. The exhibit list is ready for filing.\nL133@P4: 2. All counsel have prepared a Joint Pretrial Statement and have made a good faith effort to stipulate\nL134@P4: to as many documents, foundational requirements, etc., as reasonably possible.\nL135@P4: 3. Pretrial motions have been exchanged by all parties.\nL136@P4: 4. Once the trial starts, witnesses will be available to fully use each trial day.\nL137@P4: 5. Parties have agreed on a division of reporter fees, which are due each day before trial starts.\nL138@P4: _________________________, Atty for _________________________ Date _______________\nL139@P4: _________________________, Atty for _________________________ Date _______________\nL140@P4: \nL141@P4: _________________________, Atty for _________________________ Date _______________\nL142@P4: _________________________, Atty for _________________________ Date _______________\nL143@P4-5: _________________________, Atty for _________________________ Date _______________ 6\nL144@P5: Revised: December 2021\nL145@P5: TRIAL PROCEDURES AND INSTRUCTIONS\nL146@P5: 1. INSTRUCTIONS FOR PREPARING THE EXHIBITS:\nL147@P5: The Court requests that counsel prepare a joint exhibit list. Exhibit numbers, not letters,\nL148@P5: must be used, and no exhibit number may be used more than once. Each side’s exhibits\nL149@P5: should use a separate block of numbers. (For example, Petitioner 1-20; Respondent 21-\nL150@P5: 40.) A blank exhibit list form is attached for your convenience. Each exhibit shall have a\nL151@P5: number tab.\nL152@P5: The Court-provided exhibit list form, rather than pleading paper, is to be used.\nL153@P5: Complete the exhibit list as follows:\nL154@P5: (1) Case number\nL155@P5: (2) Case name\nL156@P5: (3) Type of hearing: Probate Trial\nL157@P5: (4) Exhibit List of: Joint\nL158@P5: (5) If you have more than one exhibit notebook, indicate which volume contains the\nL159@P5: particular exhibit.\nL160@P5: (6) In the column marked “#” type the exhibit number, beginning with number 1. Do\nL161@P5: not use letters for identifying exhibits.\nL162@P5: (7) In the column marked “Description”, include a brief, accurate description of the\nL163@P5: exhibit (e.g., “Letter of 1-18-91 from Smith to Jones”, not “Letter”), and indicate\nL164@P5: whether documents are originals or copies. See the attached sample Exhibit List.\nL165@P5: (8) Each exhibit with multiple pages shall have a Bates Labeling number on the lower\nL166@P5: right corner of each page. The bates labeling for each exhibit shall be numbered\nL167@P5: consecutively starting with the number 1. The numbers may be handwritten or\nL168@P5: typed.\nL169@P5: 2. INSTRUCTIONS FOR PREPARING EXHIBIT TAGS:\nL170@P5: The Court requests that counsel prepare and attach exhibit tags to the exhibits,\nL171@P5: corresponding to the exhibit list. Four blank exhibit tags will be provided for copying and\nL172@P5: for your convenience. Exhibit tags are only required on the clerks set of exhibit binders if\nL173@P5: the matter involves an in-person trial. Remote trials.\nL174@P5: Before filling in the “Exhibit No.” make copies of the exhibit tags and complete the tags as\nL175@P5: follows:\nL176@P5-6: (1) Type an “X” in the box “Identification.”7\nL177@P6: Revised: December 2021\nL178@P6: (2) Type an “X” in the box “Joint.”\nL179@P6: (3) Type in case number.\nL180@P6: (4) Type in case name.\nL181@P6: (5) Once you have copied the tags and cut them apart, number each exhibit tag to\nL182@P6: correspond with the exhibit list.\nL183@P6: Affix the tag to the exhibit by stapling the tags to the upper right-hand corner, of the first\nL184@P6: page. The exhibit tags are only required for the witness exhibit binder. If you have any\nL185@P6: questions concerning the exhibit list or exhibit tags, please call the clerk in the trial\nL186@P6: department.\nL187@P6: 3. EXHIBIT NOTEBOOK/BINDERS:\nL188@P6: a. The Court’s Digital Evidence Portal is now being used by some probate\nL189@P6: departments. If used in your case, the Digital Evidence Portal will be the location\nL190@P6: for the official court exhibits. Exhibit notebook/binders will still be required until\nL191@P6: further notice.\nL192@P6: b. No exhibit or trial notebooks may be more than 2 inches thick. If you have more\nL193@P6: than five 2-inch notebooks of trial exhibits, the Court requests that you provide a\nL194@P6: digital copy of the exhibits.\nL195@P6: c. Parties are encouraged to use a heavy duty 3 hole punch with 13/32” holes.\nL196@P6: d. Please provide a complete set of your exhibit notebooks to each opposing counsel\nL197@P6: or self-represented litigant, a complete set for the court, and a complete set for the\nL198@P6: witness if live in-court testimony is taken. THE CLERK DOES NOT NEED A SET IF THE\nL199@P6: DIGITAL EVIDENCE PORTAL IS BEING USED.\nL200@P6: Note: Utilization of the term “joint” in both tags and list is not to be taken as a\nL201@P6: waiver of the right to object to the admissibility of any exhibit at trial.\nL202@P6: e. The outside edge or spine of the binder should identify whether it is\nL203@P6:  petitioner’s binder or respondent’s binder. The range numbers for the\nL204@P6:  exhibits in the binder should also be visible on the outside edge or\nL205@P6:  spine of the exhibit binder and on the front cover of the notebook/binder.\nL206@P6: 4. WITNESSES:\nL207@P6: a. It isthe responsibility of counsel to arrange forthe appearance of witnessesto avoid\nL208@P6: delay caused by witnesses who have been placed on call, to confer between or\nL209@P6: among themselves during the trial as to when witnesses will be needed, to advise\nL210@P6: the Court at the earliest opportunity of any anticipated problems with the presence\nL211@P6: of witnesses, and to advise witnesses of the appropriate manner of testifying.\nL212@P6: b. If an expert or percipient witness will use documents otherthan exhibitsin evidence\nL213@P6-7: or marked for identification by the clerk while on the stand, counsel must arrange8\nL214@P7: Revised: December 2021\nL215@P7: forthe prior examination by all other counsel ofsuch documents during a time court\nL216@P7: is not in session, to avoid delay.\nL217@P7: 5. TRIALS:\nL218@P7: a. The Court expects counsel, parties and witnesses to be prompt and to maintain\nL219@P7: proper courtroom demeanor, and to be appropriately dressed (no tank tops or\nL220@P7: shorts).\nL221@P7: b. Witnesses will not engage in any direct or indirect communication with anyone\nL222@P7: during their testimony other than those communications made on the record. No\nL223@P7: one shall be present in the room in which the person is testifying other than those\nL224@P7: known and authorized by the court.\nL225@P7: c. Witnesses shall not refer to any notes or other resource materials other than what\nL226@P7: is marked as an exhibit and available to all parties and the court.\nL227@P7: d. Remote hearings via Zoom require appropriate dress and appearance.\nL228@P7: e. All witnesses other than children are to be addressed by last name.\nL229@P7: f. Objections, statements, and arguments are to be addressed to the Court rather\nL230@P7: than to opposing counsel.\nL231@P7: g. All parties are responsible for paying in advance all reporter per diem fees each day\nL232@P7: of trial.\nL233@P7: h. If a document needs to be sent to the court during the trial, the parties shall use the\nL234@P7: email address – probatecourtroom@occourts.org. This email is to be used only\nL235@P7-8: after the trial begins.SAMPLE\nL236@P8: JOINT EXHIBIT LIST\nL237@P8: Case #: (1) A123456\nL238@P8: Case Name: (2) Matter of the Estate of Smith\nL239@P8: Court Clerk: __________________________\nL240@P8: Type of Hearing: (3) Probate Trial\nL241@P8: Exhibit List of: (4) JOINT\nL242@P8:  Prior Hearing #\nL243@P8: LOC ID # EX DESCRIPTION\nL244@P8: (5) (6) (7)\nL245@P8: Vol. 1 1 Copy of 1/31/90 letter to Smith from Jones\nL246@P8: 2 Copy of Agreement dated 2/3/90 between Smith\nL247@P8: 3\nL248@P8: Original Promissory Note for $123,456.00 dated\nL249@P8: 2/6/90\nL250@P8: 4 Copy of Trust Deed dated 2/6/90\nL251@P8: Vol. 2 5 15 original canceled checks from Jones to Smith\nL252@P8: 6 Copy of Notice of Default dated 3/19/92\nL253@P8: 7 Copy of Notice of Sale\nL254@P8: 8 3 photos of real property in foreclosure\nL255@P8: Date Received:_____________________________ Received By:_____________________\nL256@P8: 1._______________________ 2._____________________ 3.________________________\nL257@P8-9: Page__ of __JOINT EXHIBIT LIST\nL258@P9: Case #: _____________________________\nL259@P9: Case Name: __________________________\nL260@P9: Court Clerk: __________________________\nL261@P9: Type of Hearing: Probate Trial\nL262@P9: Exhibit List of: JOINT\nL263@P9:  Prior Hearing #\nL264@P9: LOC ID # EX DESCRIPTION\nL265@P9: Date Received:_____________________________ Received By:_____________________\nL266@P9: 1._______________________ 2._____________________ 3.________________________\nL267@P9:  Page__ of __\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTrial Expectations and Pretrial Orders (https://www.occourts.org/system/files/general/cm03_standing_order.pdf)\nciteturn1256view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 4; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1239view3\",\"id\":320}); Total lines: 270\nL0@P0: oO\nL1@P0: =W\nL2@P0: 11\nL3@P0: 12\nL4@P0: 13\nL5@P0: 14\nL6@P0: 15\nL7@P0: 16\nL8@P0: 17\nL9@P0: 18\nL10@P0: 19\nL11@P0: 20\nL12@P0: 21\nL13@P0: 22\nL14@P0: 23\nL15@P0: 24\nL16@P0: 25\nL17@P0: 26\nL18@P0: 27\nL19@P0: 28\nL20@P0: SUPERIOR COURT OF CALIFORNIA, COUNTY OF ORANGE\nL21@P0: COSTA MESA JUSTICE COMPLEX\nL22@P0: TRIAL EXPECTATIONS AND PRETRIAL ORDERS\nL23@P0: Judge Erin Rowe\nL24@P0: Department CM03\nL25@P0: TO ALL ATTORNEYS AND SELF-REPRESENTED PARTIES ASSIGNED FOR TRIAL IN\nL26@P0: DEPARTMENT CM03\nL27@P0: The trial court has inherent and statutory discretion to control the proceedings to ensure the\nL28@P0: effective administration of justice. (People v. Gonzalez (2006) 38 Cal.4th 932, 951.) This includes the\nL29@P0: duty to keep the trial within the bounds of the issues, see that both sides receive a fair trial, and that\nL30@P0: justice is done. (People v. Alfaro (1976) 61 Cal.App.3rd 414, 421-424, 427; People v. Williams\nL31@P0: (2009) 170 Cal.App.4th 587, 615.) The following orders are to inform attorneys and self-represented\nL32@P0: parties of the court’s expectations in presenting their cases in Department CM03.\nL33@P0: 1. Expectations of Counsel and Self-Represented Parties.\nL34@P0: The court expects all attorneys and self-represented parties to act in a professional manner,\nL35@P0: and to follow the Orange County Bar Association Civility Guidelines, which may be found at\nL36@P0: https://www.ocbar.org/.\nL37@P0: 2. Pre-Trial Responsibilities of Attorneys and Self-Represented Parties.\nL38@P0: All pretrial documents are to be filed at least five court days before trial. Attorneys and/or\nL39@P0: self-represented parties are ordered to comply with Civil Local Rule 317 and all Probate Hearing and\nL40@P0: Trial Guidelines. Civil Local Rule 317 is posted on the Orange County Superior Court’s Public\nL41@P0: Website at https://www.occourts.org/ under Local and State Rules of Court, Division 3, Chapter 4.\nL42@P0: The Probate Hearing and Trial Guidelines are also posted on the Orange County Superior Court’s\nL43@P0: ate\nL44@P0: RULING AND PROPOSED STATEMENT OF DECISION\nL45@P0: ON MATTER TAKEN UNDER SUBMISSION ON JULY 11, 2024\nL46@P0: oO\nL47@P0: =W\nL48@P0: 11\nL49@P0: 12\nL50@P0: 13\nL51@P0: 14\nL52@P0: 15\nL53@P0: 16\nL54@P0: 17\nL55@P0: 18\nL56@P0: 19\nL57@P0: 20\nL58@P0: 21\nL59@P0: 22\nL60@P0: 23\nL61@P0: 24\nL62@P0: 25\nL63@P0: 26\nL64@P0: 27\nL65@P0: 28\nL66@P0: SUPERIOR COURT OF CALIFORNIA, COUNTY OF ORANGE\nL67@P0: COSTA MESA JUSTICE COMPLEX\nL68@P0: TRIAL EXPECTATIONS AND PRETRIAL ORDERS\nL69@P0: Judge Erin Rowe\nL70@P0: Department CM03\nL71@P0: TO ALL ATTORNEYS AND SELF-REPRESENTED PARTIES ASSIGNED FOR TRIAL IN\nL72@P0: DEPARTMENT CM03\nL73@P0: The trial court has inherent and statutory discretion to control the pro